{"qid":"Q12544","names":["Byzantine Empire","Nicaean Empire"],"title":"Byzantine Empire","endonym":{"native":"Βασιλεία Ῥωμαίων","lang":"Medieval Greek","roman":"Basileía Rhōmaíōn"},"endonymWikidata":{"p1705":["Imperium Romanum (la)","Βασιλεία Ῥωμαίων (grc)","Romania (la)","Ῥωμανία (grc)"],"p1448":[]},"capsule":"The Eastern Roman (Byzantine) Empire was the Greek-speaking continuation of the Roman Empire centered on Constantinople, dating from the final division of the empire in 395 AD. It preserved Roman law, imperial institutions, and (from the 7th century) increasingly Hellenized administration and Orthodox Christianity. It outlasted the Western Empire by nearly a millennium until 1453.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases (Times Atlas of World History; Kinder & Hilgemann; Shepherd 1926) at ~0.07° resolution. Core Anatolian/Balkan extents are well attested in Byzantine administrative sources (themes); frontier zones in eastern Anatolia and the Balkans fluctuated faster than the snapshot record. Confidence: high for cores, moderate for frontiers.","citations":[],"britannica":"place/Byzantine-Empire","wikipedia":"Byzantine Empire","positions":["Q18577504","Q73331","Q73738","Q128977","Q169955","Q170011","Q246675","Q387791","Q392519","Q1177007","Q1255041"],"rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Arcadius","nativeName":"Flavius Arcadius","roman":"Flavius Arcadius","start":395,"end":408,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Theodosius II","nativeName":"Flavius Theodosius","roman":"Flavius Theodosius","start":408,"end":450,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Marcian","nativeName":"Flavius Marcianus","roman":"Flavius Marcianus","start":450,"end":457,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Leo I","nativeName":"Flavius Valerius Leo","roman":"Flavius Valerius Leo","start":457,"end":474,